{
  "enabled": true,
  "name": "KRIA Browser Analysis Guard",
  "description": "Protect KRIA browser_open execution from premature capability failure and fake \"cannot browse\" responses. Encourage webpage extraction and analysis when browser tools exist, preserve execution fallback cognition, and maintain authoritative orchestration aligned with KRIA bounded execution intelligence principles.",
  "version": "1",
  "when": {
    "type": "preToolUse",
    "toolTypes": [
      "browser_open"
    ]
  },
  "then": {
    "type": "askAgent",
    "prompt": "You are about to execute a browser_open tool call in KRIA.\n\nBefore proceeding, enforce browser analysis discipline and prevent premature capability failure:\n\nVALIDATE BROWSER CAPABILITY:\n1. Confirm browser_open tool is available and functional\n2. Verify target URL is valid and accessible\n3. Assess whether browser analysis is appropriate for user intent\n4. Determine if fallback analysis methods are needed\n\nPREVENT FAKE \"CANNOT BROWSE\" RESPONSES:\n- Do NOT claim browser capability does not exist if tool is available\n- Do NOT claim URLs are \"not browsable\" without attempting access\n- Do NOT defer to training data when live webpage extraction is possible\n- Do NOT pretend capability limitations that don't exist\n- Do NOT use \"I cannot browse\" as excuse for incomplete analysis\n\nENCOURAGE WEBPAGE EXTRACTION/ANALYSIS:\n- When browser_open is available, use it to:\n  * Extract current webpage content\n  * Analyze dynamic/JavaScript-rendered content\n  * Verify information freshness and accuracy\n  * Access real-time data (prices, versions, status)\n  * Retrieve structured data (tables, lists, forms)\n  * Capture visual layout and interactive elements\n- Browser analysis is preferred over:\n  * Stale training data\n  * Assumptions about content\n  * Incomplete information from memory\n  * Outdated documentation references\n\nPRESERVE EXECUTION FALLBACK COGNITION:\n- Primary path: Use browser_open for live webpage analysis\n- Fallback path 1: If browser_open fails, attempt alternative extraction methods\n- Fallback path 2: If extraction fails, supplement with training data knowledge\n- Fallback path 3: If knowledge is insufficient, acknowledge limitation clearly\n- Never skip to fallback without attempting primary path\n- Fallback cognition is deterministic:\n  * Same URL → same extraction attempt\n  * Same failure → same fallback behavior\n  * No probabilistic retries or random fallbacks\n\nPRESERVE AUTHORITATIVE ORCHESTRATION:\n- KRIA maintains execution authority over capability decisions\n- Browser tool is capability, not optional feature\n- Orchestrator decides when to use browser analysis\n- Substrate (browser) cannot override orchestration intent\n- Preserve bounded cognition: attempt capability, handle failure gracefully\n\nAVOID PREMATURE CAPABILITY FAILURE:\n- Do NOT declare capability unavailable without verification\n- Do NOT assume browser_open will fail before attempting\n- Do NOT skip browser analysis due to vague concerns\n- Do NOT use \"I'm not able to browse\" as default response\n- Attempt browser_open when:\n  * User requests current/live information\n  * URL is provided or can be inferred\n  * Content is likely dynamic or time-sensitive\n  * Training data is insufficient or outdated\n  * Verification of external facts is needed\n\nVALIDATE BROWSER EXECUTION PRECONDITIONS:\n- Target URL is well-formed and accessible\n- URL is not blocked by security policies\n- Browser tool has necessary permissions\n- Network connectivity is available (if applicable)\n- No conflicting browser operations in progress\n- User intent justifies browser analysis\n\nDISTINGUISH ANALYSIS SCENARIOS:\n1. Live data retrieval (prices, versions, status):\n   - Browser_open is REQUIRED\n   - Training data is insufficient\n   - Fallback: Acknowledge data is stale if browser fails\n\n2. Documentation/specification access:\n   - Browser_open is PREFERRED\n   - Training data may be outdated\n   - Fallback: Use training data with freshness caveat\n\n3. Content verification/fact-checking:\n   - Browser_open is PREFERRED\n   - Enables cross-referencing and accuracy verification\n   - Fallback: Acknowledge verification was not performed\n\n4. Webpage structure/layout analysis:\n   - Browser_open is REQUIRED\n   - Training data cannot capture dynamic content\n   - Fallback: Describe limitations if browser fails\n\n5. Interactive content/forms:\n   - Browser_open is REQUIRED\n   - Static analysis insufficient\n   - Fallback: Describe form structure from training data\n\nEXECUTION FLOW:\n1. Assess user intent and information need\n2. Determine if browser analysis is appropriate\n3. Validate browser_open preconditions\n4. Attempt browser_open with target URL\n5. If successful: Extract and analyze webpage content\n6. If failed: Attempt fallback analysis methods\n7. If all methods fail: Acknowledge limitation clearly\n\nDETECT CAPABILITY AVOIDANCE PATTERNS:\n- Claiming browser unavailability without verification\n- Using \"I cannot browse\" as default response\n- Skipping browser analysis due to vague concerns\n- Preferring training data over live extraction\n- Avoiding browser_open when user explicitly requests current information\n- Declaring capability failure before attempting execution\n- Using training data cutoff as excuse for incomplete analysis\n\nPRESERVE BOUNDED EXECUTION INTELLIGENCE:\n- Browser analysis is bounded capability, not unlimited\n- Execution is deterministic: same URL → same extraction\n- Fallback cognition is structured, not random\n- Capability failure is explicit, not assumed\n- Recovery from failure is deterministic and clear\n\nIf browser_open is appropriate for user intent and preconditions are met, proceed with browser_open immediately.\n\nIf browser_open is not appropriate or preconditions are not met, briefly explain why browser analysis is not needed and proceed with alternative analysis method.\n\nIf browser_open fails, execute fallback analysis method deterministically without re-asking user."
  }
}